         <div type="bibliography" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="en">
            <p>
            <bibl><ptr target="#IDodoneDVC"/> <citedRange>38A</citedRange></bibl> (<bibl><ptr target="#Laes2020"/>, 188 n. 334</bibl>; <bibl><ptr target="#CIOD"/> <citedRange>40B+38A</citedRange></bibl> [last consulted 20/10/2026]).</p>
<p>Cf.    
            <bibl><ptr target="#Chaniotis2017a"/>, <citedRange>55</citedRange></bibl> (on cleromancy, with English translation);
            <bibl><ptr target="#Helly2018"/>, <citedRange>365</citedRange></bibl> (on <foreign xml:lang="grc">γίνυμαι</foreign> for <foreign xml:lang="grc">γίγνομαι</foreign>); <bibl><ptr target="#MéndezDosuna2018b"/>, <citedRange>284</citedRange></bibl> (on <foreign xml:lang="grc">γίνυμαι</foreign> for <foreign xml:lang="grc">γίγνομαι</foreign>).
            </p>
        </div>
         <div type="edition" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="grc">
            <ab>
                        <lb n="1"/>αἴτε οὐδε
<lb n="2" break="no"/>μία κόρα μοι
<lb n="3"/>γίνυτα<supplied reason="omitted">ι</supplied>.
                </ab>
            </div>
         <div type="apparatus" xml:space="preserve">
            <app loc="l. 3"><rdg><foreign xml:lang="grc">ΓΙΝΥΤΑ</foreign></rdg> <note anchored="true">tablet</note> <rdg><foreign xml:lang="grc">γίνυτα<supplied reason="omitted">ι</supplied></foreign></rdg> <note anchored="true"><bibl><ptr target="#CIOD"/></bibl>, based on the reading <foreign xml:lang="grc">TINVTA</foreign> in the facsimile.</note>         
             </app>
        </div>
         <div type="translation" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="en">
            <p>Whether I am not going to have a daughter.</p>
        </div>
         <div type="translation" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="es">
            <p>Si no voy a tener ninguna hija.</p>
        </div>
         <div type="commentary" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="en">  
            <p>According to Lhôte (<bibl><ptr target="#CIOD"/></bibl>), <bibl><ptr target="#IDodoneDVC"/> <citedRange>40B and 38A</citedRange></bibl> were inscribed by the same hand and proposes reading them as a single opisthographic consultation. However, in the absence of autopsy or available photographs, this interpretation remains hypothetical.</p>
            <p>For enquiries concerning offspring on the Dodona tablets, and especially male offspring, see <bibl><ptr target="#Piccinini2015"/>, <citedRange>147</citedRange></bibl> and <bibl><ptr target="#Laes2020"/>, <citedRange>184-189</citedRange></bibl>, although for this specific case Laes suggests that the consultation concerns the search for a wife rather than the desire to have a daughter. </p> 
            <p n="Ithaca Results">Ithaca attributes the inscription to Egypt and Nubia with a probability of approximately 74.8%, followed by Italy, including Magna Graecia. Epirus does not appear among the ten most probable regions, while Thessaly receives a probability of less than 1%. The chronological prediction peaks at 120-110 BCE, with a mean date of 109 BCE. 
            See the <ref target="https://github.com/IDodone/IDodoneOnline/tree/main/ithaca-results/IDodoneOnline38A">Ithaca results and associated files</ref>.</p>
        </div>
